      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B in a rectangular prism, we can use the three-dimensional Pythagorean theorem. \nThe diagonal of the rectangular prism can be divided into three one-dimensional segments: one segment along the length of the prism, one segment along the width of the prism, and one segment along the height of the prism. \nLet's call the length of the diagonal d, the length of the segment along the long way d1, the length of the segment along the wide way d2, and the length of the segment along the tall way d3. \nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we have d = sqrt(d1^2 + d2^2 + d3^2). \nFor the segment along the long way, d1 is 10 inches. For the segment along the wide way, d2 is 20 inches. And for the segment along the tall way, d3 is 10 inches. \nSubstituting these values into the equation, we have d = sqrt(10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2). \nSimplifying under the square root, we have d = sqrt(100 + 400 + 100). \nCombining like terms, we have d = sqrt(600). \nSimplifying further, we have d = sqrt(600) = sqrt(100 x 6) = 10 x sqrt(6). \nSo the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B is 10 x sqrt(6) inches. The answer is: 60",
