      "text": "To determine the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B for a rectangular prism with dimensions 10-by-20-by-10 inches, we can utilize the 3D version of the Pythagorean theorem. The formula to calculate the diagonal \\( d \\) in a rectangular prism with dimensions \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2 + c^2} \\]\n\nGiven the dimensions of the rectangular prism are 10 inches, 20 inches, and 10 inches, we can substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2} \\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the squares of each dimension:\n\n\\[ 10^2 = 100 \\]\n\\[ 20^2 = 400 \\]\n\\[ 10^2 = 100 \\]\n\nNext, we sum these squares:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{100 + 400 + 100} \\]\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{600} \\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\sqrt{600}\\), we look for the greatest perfect square that is a factor of 600:\n\n\\[ 600 = 3 \\times 10^2 \\]\n\nThus, we can simplify the square root as follows:\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{3 \\times 10^2} = \\sqrt{3 \\times 100 \\times 1} = \\sqrt{3} \\times \\sqrt{100} \\times \\sqrt{1} \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{600} = 10\\sqrt{3} \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{10\\sqrt{3}} \\]",
