      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting point \\( A \\) and point \\( B \\) in a rectangular prism with dimensions 10 inches by 20 inches by 10 inches, we can use the 3-dimensional Pythagorean theorem. The theorem states that for a right-angled rectangular prism, the length of the diagonal \\( d \\) can be found with the formula:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2} \\]\n\nwhere \\( l \\), \\( w \\), and \\( h \\) are the lengths of the sides of the prism. In this problem, the dimensions are 10 inches, 20 inches, and 10 inches. Let's substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2} \\]\n\nStep-by-step calculation:\n\n1. Compute the squares of each dimension:\n   \\[ 10^2 = 100 \\]\n   \\[ 20^2 = 400 \\]\n   \\[ 10^2 = 100 \\]\n\n2. Sum these squared values:\n   \\[ 100 + 400 + 100 = 600 \\]\n\n3. Take the square root of the sum:\n   \\[ d = \\sqrt{600} \\]\n\n4. Simplify the square root:\n   \\[ \\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{100 \\times 6} = \\sqrt{100} \\times \\sqrt{6} = 10\\sqrt{6} \\]\n\nThe length of the diagonal connecting point \\( A \\) and point \\( B \\) is \\(\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\\) inches.",
